.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt DUP 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dup ,
.Nm dup2 ,
.Nm dup3
.Nd duplicate a file descriptor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn dup "int oldfd"
.Ft int
.Fn dup2 "int oldfd" "int newfd"
.Ft int
.Fn dup3 "int oldfd" "int newfd" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn dup
duplicates
.Fa oldfd
onto the lowest unused descriptor.
The descriptors share one open-file description, including the file
offset and the
.Dv O_APPEND
and
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
status flags.
Closing one descriptor leaves the other usable.
The new descriptor's
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
flag is clear.
.Pp
.Fn dup2
duplicates
.Fa oldfd
onto
.Fa newfd .
If
.Fa newfd
is open, its previous backing references are released first;
close errors are ignored.
If both arguments are equal and valid, the call returns
.Fa newfd
without changing it, including its close-on-exec flag.
Otherwise the new descriptor's
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
flag is clear.
.Pp
.Fn dup3
performs the same replacement, but equal descriptors fail with
.Er EINVAL .
The
.Fa flags
argument must be 0 or
.Dv O_CLOEXEC .
The latter sets
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
on the new descriptor.
.Pp
The descriptor table starts with 16 entries and grows to at most 1024.
The soft
.Dv RLIMIT_NOFILE
limit is one greater than the highest new descriptor allowed.
For
.Fn dup2
and
.Fn dup3 ,
a negative destination or one at or above this limit gives
.Er EBADF .
.Pp
An open-file description releases a pipe, terminal, pseudo-terminal,
or other operations-backed object when its last reference closes.
Socket references are counted separately for each descriptor;
each duplicate adds a reference.
Replacing an open destination also releases any framebuffer ownership
associated with it.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 41 for
.Fn dup ,
63 for
.Fn dup2 ,
and 330 for
.Fn dup3 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return the new descriptor on success.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/dup2test.c
opens
.Pa dup2test.tmp ,
duplicates its descriptor onto standard output, writes
.Ql Hello from dup2! ,
and reads the file back to check the redirection.
.Pa tests/ofdprobe.c
checks that reads through duplicate descriptors share an offset and
that a duplicate remains usable after the original closes.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa oldfd
is invalid, or
.Fa newfd
is negative or at or above the descriptor limit.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fn dup3
was given equal descriptors or flags other than
.Dv O_CLOEXEC .
.It Bq Er EMFILE
.Fn dup
cannot allocate a free descriptor below the limit, or storage for a
descriptor or an enlarged table could not be allocated.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr close 2 ,
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr fcntl 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr prlimit64 2
